commit c9a9a3d776582d7cffe81448911d66972ed4fcc7
Author: Alessio Pollero <alessio.pollero@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 10 21:16:44 2026 +0400

    Skip the checkpoint components a model does not have (#711)
    
    * Skip the checkpoint components a model does not have
    
    Loading a multimodal checkpoint through LLMModelFactory reads every
    tensor, and then the text model's sanitize drops the vision and audio
    encoders. The dropped tensors go back to the MLX buffer cache, where
    they stay until generation clears it on its first token. The Gemma 4
    VLM does the same with the audio encoder it does not run, and Qwen 3.5
    targets do it with the MTP head that only the drafter loads.
    
    #580 lets a model declare its components, so the loader can skip the
    ones that load on first use. This extends it to the components a model
    does not have. A model family now declares its components once, as a
    CheckpointLayout, and every model of the family returns that layout
    from ModelComponentsProviding. The loader handles each declared part in
    one of three ways:
    
    - On demand: the model lists the component in onDemandComponents. It
      loads on first use, as before.
    - Built in: the model has the part's module. It loads with the model.
    - Absent: the model has neither. The loader does not read its tensors.
    
    So model files hold no namespace strings and no per-model exclusion
    lists, and a new model of a family behaves correctly by returning the
    family's layout:
    
    - Gemma4Checkpoint: Gemma4Model (LLM), Gemma4 and Gemma4Unified (VLM),
      and EmbeddingGemma2, which no longer needs its config-dependent
      component and exclusion lists
    - Qwen35CheckpointPolicy: Qwen35Model and Qwen35TextModel (LLM) and
      Qwen35 (VLM). The targets exclude only the MTP head now, through
      excludedCheckpointNamespaces (#632).
    - MuseGlimmerCheckpoint replaces the inline list in MuseGlimmer
    
    excludedComponents applies only to on-demand components, as its
    documentation already said. Excluding .vision on the Gemma 4 VLM, whose
    encoder is built in, loads it as before instead of failing.
    Preparation still drops these names, so a checkpoint loads into the
    same model as before.
    
    Some tensors are still read and then released: sanitize drops a few,
    and preparation replaces others. Qwen 3.5 fuses its GatedDeltaNet input
    projections after the load, and the 1.3 GB of source projections stayed
    in the cache. loadWeights now releases the checkpoint before it
    materializes the model, and materializeModelForInference clears the
    buffer cache.
    
    Measured on an M4 Pro with a 4k prompt, two alternating runs each. The
    first number is the footprint after load, the second is the peak during
    prefill:
    
    - gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit LLM: 5.43 -> 4.49 GB, 6.71 -> 6.08 GB
    - gemma-4-e4b-it-4bit VLM: 5.44 -> 4.82 GB, 6.76 -> 6.46 GB
    - gemma-4-12B-it-qat-OptiQ-4bit (gemma4_unified) LLM: 9.25 -> 9.17 GB,
      11.88 -> 11.80 GB
    - Qwen3.5-9B-8bit LLM: 11.93 -> 10.97 GB, 14.04 -> 11.99 GB
    - Qwen3.5-9B-8bit VLM, 1k prompt: 11.01 -> 10.92 GB, MLX peak
      10.81 -> 9.57 GB
    - Qwen3-14B-4bit (text only): 8.41 -> 8.41 GB, 12.93 -> 12.92 GB
    - Muse-Glimmer-30B-4bit VLM, 1k prompt: 15.88 -> 15.83 GB
    
    Greedy outputs are identical, and time to first token and decode speed
    are within run-to-run noise.
    
    * Share checkpoint components as values and warn when excludedComponents cannot skip a built-in component

