commit ec4c966a663895cd052a9c58d06a1e6f3d96d01b
Author: Alessio Pollero <alessio.pollero@gmail.com>
Date:   Tue Nov 10 09:29:21 2026 +0400

    Batch greedy MTP verification into one GPU readback per round (#724)
    
    The MTP verify loop sampled draft rows one at a time: each row cost an argmax kernel plus an eval/item GPU-to-CPU sync, so a round with K drafts stalled the stream up to K+2 times.
    
    For greedy sampling with a stateless processor (nil or SuppressTokensProcessor), verification is now batched: argmax over the whole block, one readback of the draft and target tokens together, then the acceptance scan on CPU. Other samplers and stateful processors keep the per-row loop, since their didSample updates can change later rows. Requested log probabilities come from the same processed rows the batched argmax reads.
    
    Parity is exact: pure-function parity against a sequential reference (ties, dtypes, token suppression), iterator token/cache/telemetry parity on a scripted model across block sizes and stop points, and token plus byte-exact cache parity on Qwen3.5-4B-4bit with the MTP-1 drafter.
    
    Measured on Apple M2: verify stage 1.25-6.4x faster for block sizes 2-8; end-to-end 128-token greedy generation on Qwen3.5-4B-4bit (block 2) 27.7 to 28.5 tok/s. Larger draft blocks benefit more.

